    <name>
      <text>3.3.1.3 Task: Degree of coefficient of linear</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p class="tb-exercise">Exercise</p>
<p>A polynomial \(p(x)\) satisfies</p>
<p>\[{@pol@} = (x + {@dd@})\,p(x).\]</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
  <li><p>What is the degree of \(p(x)\)?</p>
    <div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
  <li><p>What is the coefficient of \(x\) in \(p(x)\)?</p>
    <div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>When polynomials are multiplied, their <strong>degrees add</strong>, because the highest-power terms multiply to give the highest-power term of the product.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
  <li><p>The left-hand side is a quadratic, so it has degree 2. The factor \(x + {@dd@}\) has degree 1. So \(1 + \deg p = 2\), which gives \(\deg p = 1\): \(p(x)\) is linear.</p></li>
  <li><p>Write \(p(x) = mx + c\). The \(x^2\) term of \((x + {@dd@})(mx + c)\) is \(mx^2\), and it must match the \(x^2\) term \({@aa*x^2@}\) on the left. So \(m = {@aa@}\). (In fact \(p(x) = {@px@}\), and you can check that \((x + {@dd@})({@px@}) = {@pol@}\).)</p></li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
